# Round nodebatch, third sitting (kind profile): the ws+Spark decode rate under the DSpark draft against ws alone under
# the same draft. The second sitting (nodebatch-decode-lock, 2026-11-05 07:40) read the node arm's warm step at
# 26.4..27.0 ms (last10 p50) against plan (a)'s 19.4..19.7 ms with the host set locked (the decode steps fault no
# more: the lock smoke's majflt 10 over 159 steps), so the gap is the node's round trip a a layer: 7 ms a step =
# 40 x ~175 us, the top of the sparkr3-a band. A plain step pays a once a position. A DSpark pass pays a once for
# its 1 + accepts positions (the 11-05 06:02 smoke over the node: 32 positions in 22 passes, a 0.603), so the draft
# amortizes a, and the question is whether that flips the sign against ws alone under the same draft.
# [derived] plan (a) under the draft: a two-row pass's card front is the one-row's 17 ms plus the second row's
# attention and the routed experts the two rows do not share (1.0..1.5 x), 19.4..29 ms a pass, 1.45 positions a pass
# (32/22) -> 50..75 tok/s(positions). The node arm: the same pass plus a x 40 = 7 ms -> 26.4..36 ms a pass -> 40..55
# tok/s(positions). The ratio node / plain = pass_a / (pass_a + 7) = 0.73..0.81: the draft does not flip the sign; it
# raises both. The measured value is the node arm's tok/s(positions); the plan (a) arm beside it is the paired
# reference, and the ratio is the number read against 0.73..0.81.
kind: profile
question: under the DSpark draft on both arms, does the ws with the Spark decode at, above or below ws alone, and does
  the draft's amortization of the node round trip move the ratio above the plain decode's 0.58 (27.0 / 46.0 p50)?
term: tok/s(positions) @ n=96, prose P 512, depth 512, A6000 stage, the draft on the 3090 as a holder in both arms
  (BLOOMERY_DRAFT=dspark BLOOMERY_DSPARK_CARD=3090); the node arm --place bp with the Spark tier over tcp
  (BLOOMERY_TIER_NODES, BLOOMERY_TIER_LINK=tcp, BLOOMERY_HOST_LOCK=1), the plain arm --place a; both under
  BLOOMERY_CARD_BUDGET=90G, the prompt as one batch, residency unset (mid on both); the one-card runner mode (the
  two-card mode refuses an a arm under the draft)
unit: tok/s
predict: 40..55
condition: one binary (the nodebatch tree), every arm's load its own process; the Spark's tier_server loop up from the
  same tree, its caches dropped before the sitting, free >= 100 GB; no [cpu-busy] on a counted row; rows marked
  [cold] stand with that mark; 4 rounds
decide-in: the ratio node / plain inside 0.73..0.81 says a is the whole gap and the draft only scales it: the lever is
  a itself (profile the link with the engine's stamps, BLOOMERY_PROFILE=1, before any other change) or a pass with
  more rows; write both arms' positions rates beside the plain sitting's in the morning report
decide-below: below 40: the draft's two rows double the node's served ids and the node leg grows past the card front
  (read the node arm's host_slots and its `time pass` rows against the plain sitting's 26.5 ms); the draft is off
  the node path until the node's leg is read
decide-above: above 55: the two-row pass costs less than 1.0 x on the card or a is amortized further than a pass
  (the node answers both rows in one trip); write the ws+Spark draft headline with its interval and run the plan
  (a) arm's draft number as the ws-alone draft headline beside it
